User = the human asking questions.
Assistant = the AI model's previous responses.

The "Assistant" messages are simply the answers that your Agent already stored in history.

Why use "assistant"?

Because that's what most chat-oriented LLMs—including Ollama chat models and OpenAI chat models—expect.

They don't just want text; they want to know who said it.


One important point

The Agent is not becoming the Assistant.
The Agent is simply acting like a secretary that says:
"Here is everything the User said, and here is everything the Assistant replied."
Then it hands that conversation to the model.
So you can think of the Agent's job as:

History
↓
Convert history into
User / Assistant messages
↓
Send to Model

That's why "Assistant" is just a label in the conversation, not a new object in your program.

{
    "question": "What is ndarray?",
    "answer": "An ndarray is..."
}
↓
[
    {"role": "user", "content": "What is ndarray?"},
    {"role": "assistant", "content": "An ndarray is..."},
]


Imp
The Model is aware of the conversation, but it does not build the conversation.


-State Manager → Agent
-Compute Engine → Model

The compute engine doesn't own state.


exchange        = one user question + one assistant answer.
message         = one turn (either a user message or an assistant message).
conversation    = an ordered collection of messages.


             history.json
                  │
                  ▼
              History
             /       \
            /         \
           ▼           ▼
       Cache      Conversation
           \           /
            \         /
             ▼       ▼
             LearningAgent
                   │
                   ▼
               OllamaModel
                   │
                   ▼
                  LLM


