***************************

BM25 (Best Matching 25) is a highly efficient, keyword-based text retrieval and ranking mechanism. 
It is the industry standard algorithm for traditional lexical (keyword) search, famously powering the core search engine inside 
Elasticsearch, OpenSearch, and Lucene. 

## How BM25 Works (The Mechanics)
BM25 is an evolution of the classic TF-IDF (Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency) algorithm. When you submit a search query, 
BM25 calculates a relevance score for every document based on three core factors: 

* Term Frequency (TF): How many times does the search keyword appear in this document? (More appearances = higher score). 
* Inverse Document Frequency (IDF): How rare is this keyword across the entire database? Rare words (like a specific kernel error code 
  like queue_work_on) get a massive score boost, while common words (like the, error, or null) are heavily penalized.  
* Document Length Normalization: Long documents naturally contain more words. BM25 penalizes long documents so that a short, concise 
  document containing your exact keyword ranks higher than a massive log file that just happened to mention the word once. 

## A Systems Analogy for a Kernel Engineer
Think of BM25 like a highly optimized, multi-threaded grep -c wrapper that doesn't just find matching lines, but automatically sorts 
the output files by mathematical relevance.

Instead of doing complex matrix math on GPUs (like dense vector embeddings), BM25 relies on an Inverted Index—a highly efficient, 
compressed hash map on disk where the keys are words and the values are lists of document IDs containing those words. This makes BM25 
incredibly fast and lightweight on CPU resources.

## Why BM25 is Critical in Modern RAG
Even though dense vectors (like OpenAI or Chroma) understand the "meaning" of sentences, they are notoriously bad at finding exact 
strings, serial numbers, error hex codes, or variable names.

Because of this, modern enterprise RAG architectures use a Hybrid Search Pipeline:

   1. BM25 grabs documents with the exact code snippets or keywords (Sparse Retrieval).
   2. Dense Embeddings grab documents with the conceptual meaning (Dense Retrieval).
   3. A Reranker combines both lists into a final top-K payload for the LLM. 

*****************************

Yes, you can absolutely call your RAG architecture an Agent, but to be technically precise within modern AI engineering, it depends 
on how much autonomy you give the system. If the system follows a fixed, linear pipeline—meaning it always takes a query, always runs 
a vector search, and always generates an answer—it is a RAG Pipeline. If the system uses an LLM to dynamically decide whether it needs 
to search the database, what keywords to search for, or how to validate its own answer, it becomes a RAG Agent. Here is how a standard 
RAG pipeline transforms into an Autonomous Agent using your package framework:

1. The Transformation: Pipeline vs. Agent
1.1 The Linear RAG Pipeline (Deterministic).

The execution flow is hardcoded in your main.py:
User Query ──> [Run Loader] ──> [Run Retriever] ──> [Feed LLM] ──> Output

The LLM has no choice; it is simply the final text synthesizer at the end of an assembly line.

1.2 The Autonomous RAG Agent (Dynamic)
The LLM sits at the core of the execution loop and controls your plugin framework like a set of peripheral tools:
               ┌───────────────┐
               │   User Query  │
               └───────┬───────┘
                       │
                       ▼
             /───────────────────\
            <  LLM Reasoning Loop > ◄─── Memory Buffer
             \───────────────────/
               /       │       \
              ▼        ▼        ▼
       [Tool: BM25] [Tool: Chroma] [Tool: Web API]

