|              |                                                       |                                                            |
| scikit-learn | `TfidfVectorizer` and cosine similarity               |                                                            |

Notes:
    1. en_core_web_sm is a model—specifically, it is a small, pre-trained English language pipeline developed for the spaCy NLP library.
    2. Breakdown of the Model Name
           The name follows spaCy's strict naming convention to tell you exactly what it contains:
              en: The language of the model (English).
              core: The capabilities of the model (a core pipeline capable of handling tokenization, part-of-speech tagging, parsing, and
                    named entity recognition).
              web: The type of training data used (built using web text like blogs, news articles, and comments).
              sm: The size of the model (small). It is optimized for CPU speed and low memory usage because it does not include static
                  word vectors
    3. model downloaded and installed as a pky, hence spacy.load() can connect to model and spins up the natural language processing (NLP)
       pipeline, and connects it to your script.
    4. text (str)
         ↓
       nlp(text)
         ↓
       spaCy processing pipeline
         ↓
       Doc
         ↓
       Token  Token  Token  Token ...
    5. nlp(text) breaks it down into tokens,
       example token is;
       token.text     → "On"       # string
       token.is_stop  → True       # bool
       token.is_punct → False      # bool
       token.pos_     → "ADP"      # linguistic classification
       token.lemma_   → "on"       # lemma
ADP / PROPN :
To be precise: PROPN (Proper Noun) absolutely is a linguistic attribute.

SpaCy separates its work into two completely different categories:
    1. Linguistics
    2. Semantics

1. Linguistic Attributes (What en_core_web_sm does perfectly)
PROPN is a Part-of-Speech (POS) tag, which is a core linguistic attribute.

* It describes the structural, grammatical role a word plays in a sentence.
* The small model (sm) figures this out by looking at the word's form, suffix, capitalization, and the words surrounding it.
* It handles linguistics brilliantly without needing any heavy semantic vectors.

2. Semantic Attributes (What en_core_web_sm lacks)
Semantics is about the fundamental, conceptual "meaning" of the word isolated from its grammar.

* A vector maps things like: “A smartphone is functionally similar to a laptop, but conceptually distant from a potato.”
* The small model does not have this mathematical understanding of meaning.

The Breakdown
To see how a single token has linguistic properties but lacks semantic properties in the small model, look at this breakdown:

| Attribute Type | Property in spaCy  | What it tells you                            | en_core_web_sm Capability                |
|----------------|--------------------|----------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|
| Linguistic     | token.pos_ (PROPN) | Grammatically, this is a specific name.      | Yes (100% functional)                    |
| Linguistic     | token.dep_ (nsubj) | This word is the subject of the verb.        | Yes (100% functional)                    |
| Semantic       | token.vector       | The mathematical coordinates of its meaning. | No (It returns an empty/pruned array)    |

Right to view PROPN as a linguistic attribute! The small model is a powerhouse for structural linguistics — it just leaves out the heavy
semantic vector math to save memory.


|                 | spaCy vector                             | BM25                              |
| --------------- | ---------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Representation  | Dense                                    | Sparse                            |
| Main signal     | Semantic                                 | Lexical                           |
| Based on        | learned vector representation            | words/terms and their frequencies |
| Dimensions      | fixed model dimension                    | vocabulary size                   |
| Most dimensions | generally non-zero                       | mostly zero                       |


Dense → semantic meaning
Sparse → lexical words


Feature   |         Lexical                  |          Linguistic                      |
----------|----------------------------------|------------------------------------------|
Focus     | Vocabulary                       | The entire system of communication       |
Component | Words, roots, prefixes, suffixes | Syntax, phonetics, semantics, morphology |
Analogy   | Entries in a dictionary          | The rules of the whole language          |


WordNet
   ↓
multiple synsets for a word
   ↓
each synset has lemmas
   ↓
same lemma may appear repeatedly

